   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 18924700,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 2344005,
    "poster_id": 167643,
    "post_rendered": "Given an isosceles triangle <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\"  width=\"58\" height=\"13\" >,</span> <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/9/3c99bbe74cccdc92b249d376f13d9429d41a8b7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=AC$\"  width=\"80\" height=\"13\" >.</span> A line passes through <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\"  width=\"19\" height=\"12\" >,</span> the midpoint of <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" >,</span> and intersects segment <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" > and ray <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e657259c0995d2c36d46744bb4aa81471e6575ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"13\" > at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\"  width=\"15\" height=\"12\" > and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" >,</span> respectively. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\"  width=\"14\" height=\"12\" > be a point of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/a/d1a30c9c946a561bfb130894432aa55948d58b0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ME$\"  width=\"33\" height=\"12\" > such that <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/3/db38ef5ad0fbbef438ab3e93a9e3ef00f3e3dc7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$EF=DM$\"  width=\"87\" height=\"12\" >,</span> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb064112b6c94470339f6571f69d07afc1c024c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K$\"  width=\"16\" height=\"12\" > be a point on <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/f/f2f00f55b6b1cf59b8a3b3687a2b9aed8b37578c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$MD$\"  width=\"34\" height=\"12\" >.</span> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/0/d002c517784078b5834f12efe45ad224596f75ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Gamma_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" > be the circle passes through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/2/c/42cf34c57fb6788c76f526321c1a799f579e7cca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B,D,K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"63\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/6/1169b5ab2cbc837edbec8298a9a56d23dfedca02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Gamma_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" > be the circle passes through <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/e/52e17da79ee4c3a1deb6fda76e93c12a70ff7102.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C,E,K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"60\" height=\"16\" >.</span> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/0/d002c517784078b5834f12efe45ad224596f75ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Gamma_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/6/1169b5ab2cbc837edbec8298a9a56d23dfedca02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Gamma_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" > intersect again at <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10f84beb25b60308571fe1764d544740714fcee4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L \\neq K$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"17\" >.</span> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" > be the circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/6/106fb278be53de164d4b7a36355d6f1c2f00d239.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle LDE$\"  width=\"58\" height=\"13\" > and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/8/d8862f64a908d94f0e6143f9b71b6dfc308a7902.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle LKM$\"  width=\"64\" height=\"13\" >.</span> Prove that, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cddbccf89c00e019e9878f87f8d6325697e3f2fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7cef5fa7ec053472a6e471aaacb0c5927ce36b36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" > are symmetric wrt <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/9/859ccf4cd60c7bc6b8fa1afc9a42dc811a826d6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$L$\"  width=\"12\" height=\"12\" >,</span> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/e/65e4f2d5cc59e5ffbed3734130e8c45b643adc91.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BF$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" > is perpendicular to <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\"  width=\"28\" height=\"12\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Given an isosceles triangle $\\triangle ABC$, $AB=AC$. A line passes through $M$, the midpoint of $BC$, and intersects segment $AB$ and ray $CA$ at $D$ and $E$, respectively. Let $F$ be a point of $ME$ such that $EF=DM$, and $K$ be a point on $MD$. Let $\\Gamma_1$ be the circle passes through $B,D,K$ and $\\Gamma_2$ be the circle passes through $C,E,K$. $\\Gamma_1$ and $\\Gamma_2$ intersect again at $L \\neq K$. Let $\\omega_1$ and $\\omega_2$ be the circumcircle of $\\triangle LDE$ and $\\triangle LKM$. Prove that, if $\\omega_1$ and $\\omega_2$ are symmetric wrt $L$, then $BF$ is perpendicular to $BC$.",
    "username": "parmenides51",
